db/id #edb/ref 17592186139159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139160}] :knowledge-point/title "The Quotient Rule With Cube Roots"} :step/id #uuid "c1d5b8d5-8593-5e6a-84da-d4aaea527af8" :step/math-academy-id 17376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139644 :tutorial/content "To divide square roots, we simply divide the numbers under the root. This is called the **quotient rule for radicals**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nby applying the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{2}} = \\sqrt{5}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule also works for cube roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{10}}{\\sqrt[3]{2}} = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{10}{2}} = \\sqrt[3]{5}\n$$\n\nIn general, the quotient rule works for any quotient of $n$th roots. So for any $n$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt[n]{10}}{\\sqrt[n]{2}} = \\sqrt[n]{\\dfrac{10}{2}} = \\sqrt[n]{5}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot use the quotient rule when we have the quotient of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt[3]{5}}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because the radicals $\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$ are different." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ea49118-4345-575a-a14f-9aedade211d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3297 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7e0565a6-cf26-5e4b-8bc5-c3de10c211d9" :step/math-academy-id 18177 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139305}}] :activity/title "The Quotient Rule for Radicals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138594}]}
